# The Weaver of Forgotten Dreams

In the time before memory, there lived a spider named Arachne who dwelled in the space between sleeping and waking. Unlike her earthly cousins who spun webs of silk, Arachne wove nets from the dreams that humans forgot upon waking.

Each night, she would climb the silver threads connecting the mortal world to the realm of sleep. There, she collected the abandoned dreams—the ones that slipped away like water through fingers when dawn arrived. These forgotten visions shimmered like dewdrops, and Arachne carefully gathered them in her eight delicate legs.

With infinite patience, she wove these lost dreams into a magnificent tapestry that hung in the void between worlds. In her web, you could see the warrior who dreamed of peace, the miser who dreamed of generosity, the lonely who dreamed of connection.

The gods, discovering her work, were moved. "Why do you preserve what humans discard?" they asked.

Arachne replied, "Because forgotten dreams are not lost dreams. They are seeds waiting to be remembered."

And so, when humans experience déjà vu or sudden inspiration, it is Arachne's gift—a thread from her tapestry, returning a forgotten dream to its dreamer, giving them another chance to make it real.
